{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts the cross-city variance 'is governed by the geometry of the urban canyon' and names 'greater built density and taller street walls' as the modulator, but the mechanism paragraph simultaneously attributes the larger nocturnal anomaly to 'larger, denser city with deeper canyons AND weaker ventilation' — folding in city size and wind/ventilation as co-drivers. This means no single mechanism is cleanly identified: the account is an aggregate of geometry + thermal mass + wind suppression + city scale, so it fails to answer the 'what single mechanism' part of the problem. The counterconditions further concede that anthropogenic heat or latent-heat deficit can dominate, and that wind can overwhelm canyon trapping, which undercuts that 'sky view factor / geometry' is the best single cross-city modulator.","counterexample":["A city with tall, dense canyons but strong regional winds (weak ventilation resistance) could have a SMALLER nocturnal UHI than a lower-density city with calm air and deep soil moisture deficit, contradicting 'deeper canyons => larger gap.'","Two cities with identical sky-view factors but very different anthropogenic heat output or irrigation/aridity would show different nocturnal gaps, showing geometry is not the best single predictor."],"preise":"The problem asks for THE single mechanism that best explains why the night-time gap is LARGER in some cities than in others, but the target's cross-city variance claim is a conjunction of multiple independent mechanisms (sky-view geometry, thermal mass/storage, wind suppression, city size, ventilation) rather than identifying one dominant modulator.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"target_claim","quote":"The nocturnal UHI and its cross-city variance are governed by the geometry of the urban canyon: deep, dense building arrays shrink the sky view factor, trapping longwave radiation and slowing radiative cooling, so cities with greater built density and taller street walls retain more daytime stored heat after sunset and cool more slowly."},{"block":"target_mechanism","quote":"Thus a larger, denser city with deeper canyons and weaker ventilation releases stored heat more slowly, producing a larger and more persistent nocturnal warm anomaly relative to open countryside."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}